.docname {Notification}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Breach | A reading past its limit, sent by `telemetry` | 31 °C, limit 28 °C |
    | Alert record | One breach as a Kafka message on `alerts` | An `AlertEvent` keyed `b1:r1` |
    | Cooldown | A Redis key that mutes repeats of one breach for 300 s | `alert:temperature:temperature:b1:r1` |
    | Domain | The organisation area a building belongs to. Alerts go to its members | `eng` |
    | Bell | The in-app message list in the browser, fed through `socket` | "Temperature Alert - HQ" |
    | Web Push | A pop-up the browser shows even with the tab closed | Sent through `fcm.googleapis.com` |
    | Push subscription | One device's push address and keys | `{endpoint, keys: {p256dh, auth}}` |
    | VAPID | A pair of keys that proves to the push service that the messages come from this server | `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY` |
    | Preference | One account's on/off switch for one metric in one domain | Ada, `eng`, `airQuality`, on |
    | Audience | Which domains a caller may reach | `Domains(["eng"])` or `Unrestricted` |
    | Kafka offset | The number of a record inside a topic partition | 41 |
    | Commit | Telling Kafka "everything below this offset is done" | commit 43 |
    | Dead-letter topic | A Kafka topic for records the service cannot use. Also called the DLQ | `alerts.dlq` |
    | Watermark | The highest offset below which every record is done | 41 when 42 is still running |
    | Port | A Rust `trait` the core logic talks to, so tests can swap the real thing for a fake | `PushSender`, `Cooldown` |

`notification` is a Rust service (Axum, MongoDB, Kafka, Redis) in `backend/notification`. It reads breaches from Kafka and decides **who should hear about each one**. Then it delivers every breach twice:

- to the **bell**, through Redis and `socket`;
- to **devices**, through Web Push.

It also stores devices and preferences, and sends manual alerts.

Role in the system: [Notification](../architecture/notification-architecture.qd). Breach shape and how it is parsed: [Telemetry Schema](../packages/telemetry-schema.qd). Message shape: [Notification Schema](../packages/notification-schema.qd).

---

## The idea in one minute

Imagine a fire-alarm office with a logbook.

- A sensor tells the office: "room `r1` of `b1` is at 31 °C, the limit is 28". That is a **breach**.
- The office asks: *which departments own building `b1`?* (`digital-twin` knows). For each department it does two things: pins a note on the **notice board** (the bell, which every open browser of that department shows) and sends a **text message** to the phones of the people who asked for such alerts.
- People choose which kinds of alerts they want, per department: temperature yes, air quality no. That is the **preference**.
- If the same room keeps breaching every 10 seconds, the office does not ring every 10 seconds. After one alert it stays quiet about that room and that metric for **5 minutes**. That is the **cooldown**.
- A note with no department is never pinned: it would reach every department in every company.

.mermaid
    flowchart LR
        T["telemetry"] -- "Kafka: alerts" --> N[("notification<br/>the alarm office")]
        N -- "who owns b1?" --> W["digital-twin"]
        N -- "PUBLISH notifications" --> R["Redis"]
        R --> S["socket<br/>(the bell)"]
        N -- "Web Push" --> P["push services<br/>(phones)"]
        N --- M[("MongoDB<br/>devices, preferences")]
        N -. "cooldown keys" .-> R

