## `alert_listener.rs`: reading Kafka safely

This is the most careful file in the service. The goal: **never lose a breach, even if the process dies half way**, and **never get stuck on a record that cannot be handled**.

### The consumer

```rust
let consumer: StreamConsumer = ClientConfig::new()
    .set("bootstrap.servers", brokers)
    .set("group.id", GROUP_ID)             // "alerts"
    .set("auto.offset.reset", "earliest")
    .set("enable.auto.commit", "false")
    .create()?;
consumer.subscribe(&[ALERTS_TOPIC])?;
```

- The group is named `alerts`: after **what it consumes**, not who consumes it.
- `earliest`: a group with no committed offset reads the whole topic from the start.
- **No auto commit.** Offsets advance because this service says a record is done, not because a timer fired. Without this, a crash could leave the offset past a breach that was never delivered.

Before subscribing, `ensure_topics` creates `alerts` and `alerts.dlq` (1 partition each) if they are missing, and ignores "already exists". Subscribing to a topic that does not exist waits for a metadata refresh, five minutes by default.

### The loop that never ends

```rust
pub async fn listen(brokers: &str, alerts: Arc<Alerts>) -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    loop {
        match consume(brokers, alerts.clone()).await {
            Ok(()) => log::warn!("[Event] Alert stream ended, reconnecting"),
            Err(e) => log::error!("[Event] Alert consumer failed: {e}"),
        }
        tokio::time::sleep(RECONNECT_DELAY).await;
    }
}
```

Any ending (clean or error) waits 5 seconds and starts again. A broker that is down at start is not fatal: the listener retries every 5 s.

### 16 at a time

```rust
consumer
    .stream()
    .for_each_concurrent(IN_FLIGHT, |message| { … })   // IN_FLIGHT = 16
```

Up to 16 records are handled at once. That is faster, but it means they can **finish out of order**: record 42 can end before 40. This creates a problem for the commit.

### The watermark

Committing an offset in Kafka means "everything **below** this is done". It is a line, not a list. If 42 finishes first and the service commits 42, then dies, records 40 and 41 are lost forever.

```rust
struct Partition {
    in_flight: BTreeSet<i64>,
    completed: BTreeSet<i64>,
}

fn started(&mut self, partition: i32, offset: i64) {
    self.partitions.entry(partition).or_default().in_flight.insert(offset);
}

fn finished(&mut self, partition: i32, offset: i64) -> Option<i64> {
    let state = self.partitions.entry(partition).or_default();
    state.in_flight.remove(&offset);
    state.completed.insert(offset);

    let lowest_running = state.in_flight.iter().next().copied();
    let committable: Vec<i64> = state
        .completed
        .iter()
        .take_while(|candidate| lowest_running.is_none_or(|running| **candidate < running))
        .copied()
        .collect();

    let watermark = committable.last().copied();
    for offset in committable {
        state.completed.remove(&offset);
    }
    watermark
}
```

Two sets per partition: offsets **running** and offsets **done**. When one finishes, take the done offsets that are lower than the lowest one still running. The highest of them is the new watermark. Only that run is committed.

**Worked example.** Offsets 40, 41, 42 start.

| Event | Running | Done | Commit |
|---|---|---|---|
| 42 finishes | 40, 41 | 42 | nothing: 40 and 41 still run |
| 40 finishes | 41 | 40, 42 | `40`, so the commit position is 41 (the next to read) |
| 41 finishes | none | 41, 42 | `42`, so the commit position is 43 |

```rust
fn commit(consumer: &StreamConsumer, partition: i32, watermark: i64) {
    // A committed offset names the next record to read, not the last one read.
    position.add_partition_offset(ALERTS_TOPIC, partition, Offset::Offset(watermark + 1))
    // … consumer.commit(&position, CommitMode::Async)
}
```

The `+ 1` is because Kafka stores "the next record to read".

If the service dies in between, Kafka delivers again from the last commit. The cooldown stops most of the repeats from reaching people.

### What to do with a record

```rust
enum Disposition {
    Settled,
    Park(&'static str),
}

async fn handle(alerts: &Alerts, payload: Option<&[u8]>) -> Disposition {
    let Some(raw) = payload.and_then(|bytes| std::str::from_utf8(bytes).ok()) else {
        metrics::record_alert_consumed("undecodable");
        return Disposition::Park("undecodable");
    };
    let outcome = alerts.on_breach(raw).await;
    metrics::record_alert_consumed(outcome.label());

    match outcome.label() {
        label @ ("invalid" | "failed") => Disposition::Park(label),
        _ => Disposition::Settled,
    }
}
```

- Not text, `invalid`, or `failed`: **park** it. Retrying the same record in a moment would not fix it (malformed beyond parsing, or a dependency was down).
- Everything else: settled.

```rust
if let Disposition::Park(reason) = handle(&alerts, message.payload()).await
    && let Err(e) = park(&dead_letters, &message, reason).await
{
    // Leaving the offset uncommitted is the whole point: the
    // record is still on the topic and will be redelivered.
    log::error!("[Event] Failed to park an alert in {ALERTS_DLQ_TOPIC}: {e}");
    return;
}
```

Parking copies the record to the dead-letter topic with a `reason` header (`undecodable`, `invalid` or `failed`). If the **parking itself fails**, the function returns *without* marking the offset finished. So the offset is never committed past it, and Kafka delivers it again. Better a repeat than a loss.

---

## `service/push.rs`: the devices

```rust
pub async fn to_domain(&self, payload: &Notification, domain_name: &str, notification_type: Option<&str>) {
    match self.preferences.accounts_subscribed_to(domain_name, notification_type).await {
        Ok(accounts) => self.to_accounts(payload, &accounts).await,
        Err(e) => log::error!("Failed to resolve subscribers of {domain_name}: {e:?}"),
    }
}

pub async fn to_accounts(&self, payload: &Notification, account_names: &[String]) {
    if account_names.is_empty() {
        return;
    }
    let subscriptions = match self.subscriptions.find_by_accounts(account_names).await {
        Ok(subscriptions) => subscriptions,
        Err(e) => {
            log::error!("Failed to load push subscriptions: {e:?}");
            return;
        }
    };
    join_all(subscriptions.iter().map(|subscription| self.deliver(subscription, payload))).await;
}

async fn deliver(&self, subscription: &WebPushSubscription, payload: &Notification) {
    if self.sender.send(subscription, payload).await == PushOutcome::SubscriptionGone
        && let Err(e) = self.subscriptions.delete_by_endpoint(&subscription.endpoint).await
    {
        log::error!("Failed to drop a dead push subscription: {e:?}");
    }
}
```

1. Ask which accounts want this kind of alert in this domain.
2. Load all of their devices.
3. Send to **every device at the same time** (`join_all`). One slow or dead device never holds up the others.
4. A device that answers "gone" is deleted. Any other failure leaves it in place.

| Push service answers | Outcome | Action |
|---|---|---|
| 2xx | `Delivered` | none |
| 404 or 410 | `SubscriptionGone` | delete the subscription by endpoint |
| any other status, network error, 10 s timeout, encryption error | `Failed` | log only, keep it |

A `403` means the push service refused the VAPID signature: a wrong or rotated key. Deleting on it would wipe every device at once, so it is kept.

### `adapters/driven/push.rs`: the real sending

```rust
let signer = (!public_key.is_empty() && !private_key.is_empty())
    .then(|| VapidSignatureBuilder::from_base64_no_sub(private_key))
    .transpose()
    .unwrap_or_else(|e| {
        log::error!("VAPID private key is not a valid base64url EC key: {e}");
        None
    });
if signer.is_none() {
    log::error!("VAPID keys are not configured; push notifications are disabled");
}
```

With no keys, or a broken private key, there is no signer. The service still starts: it logs the problem, and every push then fails with `Failed`. Nothing panics.

```rust
let mut signature = signer.add_sub_info(&info);
signature.add_claim("sub", SUBJECT);          // "mailto:admin@crowdvision.com"

let mut message = WebPushMessageBuilder::new(&info);
message.set_payload(ContentEncoding::Aes128Gcm, body);
message.set_vapid_signature(signature.build()?);
```

The payload (the `Notification` as JSON) is **encrypted** with the device's own keys (`aes128gcm`), so the push service cannot read it. The VAPID signature proves who is sending. Then:

```rust
let mut request = self.client.post(message.endpoint.to_string())
    .header("TTL", message.ttl.to_string());
// … content-encoding, content-type, crypto headers, body
Ok(request.send().await?.status().as_u16())
```

A plain `POST` to the device's endpoint, with a 10 second timeout on the HTTP client. The status number decides the outcome in the table above.

### The bell and the switches

- The bell message goes to the **whole domain** room and carries `metric`.
- The browser hides a breach whose metric the account switched off (`isVisible` in `frontend/src/utils/commons/notification.ts`).
- Push applies the same rule on the server, through `notificationType` in step 1.
- A manual alert has no `metric`: the bell always shows it.

---

## `adapters/driven/twin.rs`: who owns the building?

The same lookup pattern as in [Socket](socket.qd), with one difference in how it caches. Here is the whole of it.

```rust
const TIMEOUT: Duration = Duration::from_secs(2);
const TTL: Duration = Duration::from_secs(15 * 60);

async fn domains_for_building(&self, building_name: &str, claims_header: &str) -> anyhow::Result<Vec<String>> {
    if let Some(domains) = self.cached(building_name) {
        return Ok(domains);
    }

    let gate = self.gate(building_name);
    let _lookup = gate.lock().await;
    // Whoever held the gate may have just answered this exact question.
    if let Some(domains) = self.cached(building_name) {
        return Ok(domains);
    }

    let domains = self.fetch(building_name, claims_header).await?;
    if !domains.is_empty() {
        self.remember(building_name, &domains);
    }
    Ok(domains)
}
```

1. A cached answer under 15 minutes old: return it.
2. Else take the **gate** of this building. One lookup per building at a time.
3. Check the cache again (the one who held the gate may have just filled it).
4. Ask `digital-twin`. **Cache the answer only if it is not empty.**

```rust
let url = format!("{}/domain/{}", self.base_url, urlencoding::encode(building_name));
// … GET with the claims header; on a network error or 5xx, wait 50 to 100 ms and try once more
if !response.status().is_success() {
    anyhow::bail!("Twin lookup failed for building {building_name}");
}
Ok(response.json().await?)
```

The building name goes through `urlencoding::encode`, so it cannot leave the `/domain/` route. One retry on a network error or `5xx`, never on a `4xx`.

| Rule | Why |
|---|---|
| 2 s timeout | A hung `digital-twin` must fail the lookup, not stall the consumer |
| One retry on network error or 5xx only | A dropped connection should not cost an alert. A 404 will not change |
| Gate per building | 16 records for the same building at once make 1 request, not 16 |
| Cache 15 min, only non-empty answers | Ownership rarely changes. A building just being registered is asked again next time |
| Answer cleaned by `unique_non_empty` | Duplicates and empty names never become rooms |

**Example.** 5 breaches from `b1` arrive together with an empty cache. The first takes the gate and calls `digital-twin`; it answers after 300 ms. The other 4 wait on the gate, find the cache filled, and return without a call. `digital-twin` sees 1 request.

---

## Registering a device: `subscribe`

```rust
pub async fn subscribe(State(state): State<AppState>, claims: GatewayClaims, Json(body): Json<SubscribeRequest>)
    -> Result<impl IntoResponse, DomainError>
{
    let account_name = claims.account_name();
    let supplied = match body.subscription {
        Some(subscription) if subscription.endpoint.is_some() => subscription,
        _ => IncomingSubscription { endpoint: body.endpoint, keys: body.keys },
    };
    let keys = supplied.keys.unwrap_or_default();

    let subscription = WebPushSubscription::new(
        account_name, supplied.endpoint.as_deref(), keys.p256dh.as_deref(), keys.auth.as_deref(),
    )?;
    state.preferences.register_device(&subscription).await?;

    if let Some(domain_name) = domain_of(body.domain_name, body.domain_id) {
        require_membership(&claims, &domain_name)?;
        let updates = body.preferences.resolve_lenient(account_name, &domain_name)?;
        state.preferences.apply(&updates).await?;
    }

    Ok((StatusCode::CREATED, Json(json!({ "success": true }))))
}
```

In order:

1. **The account comes from the claims**, never from the body or the path. A body with `"accountName": "mallory"` sent by Ada still writes to Ada.
2. Take the subscription from the `subscription` object, else from top-level `endpoint` and `keys`. Both shapes are accepted.
3. Build and **validate** it (below). Store it (an upsert).
4. If a domain is named (`domainName` or `domainId`): check membership, then switch preferences on.
5. Answer `201 {success: true}`.

### The checks in `WebPushSubscription::new`

| Check | Fails with |
|---|---|
| Account not blank | `Invalid authenticated account` |
| `endpoint`, `p256dh`, `auth` present | `Invalid push subscription payload` |
| `endpoint` is `https`, default port, host is or ends in `.fcm.googleapis.com`, `.push.services.mozilla.com`, `.push.apple.com`, `.notify.windows.com` | `Push endpoint must be an https URL on a known push service` |

```rust
url.scheme() == "https"
    && url.port().is_none()
    && PUSH_SERVICE_HOSTS.iter().any(|known| host == *known || host.ends_with(&format!(".{known}")))
```

The server POSTs to the endpoint, so an arbitrary URL would let a caller point the service at any address (an attack called SSRF). The `.` in `ends_with(".{known}")` matters: it accepts `x.fcm.googleapis.com` but not `evilfcm.googleapis.com`.

- **The device is stored before the membership check.** A domain the caller is not in gives `403`, but the device stays registered, with no preferences for that domain.
- The upsert key is the endpoint. One browser that signs in as another account moves its endpoint to that account.

**Example.** Ada, member of `eng`, sends `{"endpoint":"https://fcm.googleapis.com/fcm/send/abc","keys":{"p256dh":"BN…","auth":"k3…"},"domainName":"eng"}` with no type. `resolve_lenient` switches on every alertable metric (`temperature`, `airQuality`, `peopleCount`). Answer `201`.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant H as subscribe handler
        participant D as WebPushSubscription::new
        participant Pr as Preferences
        participant DB as MongoDB
        B->>H: POST /subscribe {endpoint, keys, domainName}
        H->>H: GatewayClaims from x-gateway-claims
        H->>H: take subscription object, else top-level endpoint and keys
        H->>D: new(account, endpoint, p256dh, auth)
        D-->>H: ok, or 400
        H->>Pr: register_device
        Pr->>DB: find_one_and_replace by endpoint, upsert
        alt domainName or domainId given
            H->>H: require_membership(claims, domain)
            H->>H: resolve_lenient(account, domain)
            H->>Pr: apply(updates)
            Pr->>DB: set, once per type
        end
        H-->>B: 201 {success: true}

---

## Changing preferences

`POST /preferences` takes one of three body forms, all pinned in `schemas/fixtures/notification-preferences.json`:

| Form | Example | Result |
|---|---|---|
| Single | `{"domainName":"eng","type":"airQuality","enabled":false}` | 1 update |
| List | `{"domainName":"eng","types":["temperature","peopleCount"],"enabled":true}` | 2 updates |
| Granular | `{"domainName":"eng","preferences":[{"type":"temperature","enabled":true},{"type":"airQuality","enabled":false}]}` | 2 updates |

`domainId` is accepted in place of `domainName`.

### One update, validated

```rust
pub fn new(account_name: &str, domain_name: &str, notification_type: Option<&str>, enabled: bool)
    -> Result<Self, DomainError>
{
    // … account not blank, domain not empty
    let notification_type = notification_type.filter(|t| !t.is_empty()).unwrap_or(TEMPERATURE);
    if !NOTIFICATION_TYPES.contains(&notification_type) {
        return Err(DomainError::Validation(format!(
            "type must be one of: {}", NOTIFICATION_TYPES.join(", ")
        )));
    }
    // …
}
```

A missing type becomes `temperature` here. `NOTIFICATION_TYPES` **is** `ALERTABLE_METRICS` from the shared crate: one list, never a second copy in this service. So a new alertable metric needs no change here.

### Two ways to read a request

```rust
pub fn resolve_lenient(...)  // used by /subscribe
let enabled = self.enabled != Some(false);
// types given: only those. Otherwise: every alertable metric.

pub fn resolve_strict(...)   // used by POST /preferences
// an entry in preferences[] without a type -> 400 "type is required"
// types[] given: enabled is required
// single form: enabled is required, then type is required
```

- **Lenient**: for `/subscribe`. No type means every alertable metric. No `enabled` means on. Only an explicit `false` turns it off.
- **Strict**: for `POST /preferences`. A missing `type` or `enabled` is a `400`: the person must say exactly what to change.

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        B["body"] --> Dn{"domainName or domainId?"}
        Dn -- no --> E1["400 domainName is required"]
        Dn -- yes --> M{"Audience permits domain?"}
        M -- no --> E2["403"]
        M -- yes --> G{"preferences[] has an entry without type?"}
        G -- yes --> E3["400 type is required"]
        G -- no --> Gr{"preferences[] not empty?"}
        Gr -- yes --> U1["one update per entry"]
        Gr -- no --> Li{"types[] not empty?"}
        Li -- yes --> En1{"enabled given?"}
        En1 -- no --> E4["400 enabled is required"]
        En1 -- yes --> U2["one update per type"]
        Li -- no --> En2{"enabled given?"}
        En2 -- no --> E4
        En2 -- yes --> Ty{"type given?"}
        Ty -- no --> E3
        Ty -- yes --> U3["one update"]
        U1 & U2 & U3 --> V{"every type in ALERTABLE_METRICS?"}
        V -- no --> E6["400 type must be one of: temperature, airQuality, peopleCount"]
        V -- yes --> W["apply: set, one by one"]

### How one switch is written

```rust
async fn set(&self, update: &PreferenceUpdate) -> anyhow::Result<()> {
    let owner = owner(&update.account_name, &update.domain_name);

    self.collection
        .update_one(owner.clone(),
            doc! { "$pull": { "preferences": { "notificationType": &update.notification_type } } })
        .await?;

    self.collection
        .update_one(owner,
            doc! {
                "$setOnInsert": { "accountName": …, "domainName": …, "createdAt": DateTime::now() },
                "$push": { "preferences": { "notificationType": &update.notification_type, "isSubscribed": update.enabled } },
            })
        .upsert(true)
        .await?;
    Ok(())
}
```

Two writes on the one document of {account, domain}:

1. `$pull` any entry with this `notificationType`.
2. `$push` the new `{notificationType, isSubscribed}`. `upsert` creates the document on the first write, and `$setOnInsert` sets `createdAt` only then.

So writing the same switch twice leaves **one** entry. The two writes are not atomic: a crash between them leaves the type missing, which reads as off.

`apply` writes updates one after the other and **stops at the first error**, so a list can be half applied.

### Who is subscribed to what

```rust
async fn accounts_subscribed_to(&self, domain_name: &str, notification_type: Option<&str>) -> anyhow::Result<Vec<String>> {
    let mut elem_match = doc! { "isSubscribed": true };
    if let Some(notification_type) = notification_type {
        elem_match.insert("notificationType", notification_type);
    }
    // find { domainName: {$eq: domain}, preferences: {$elemMatch: elem_match} }
    // then make the account names unique
}
```

With a type: documents of the domain that have an element `{isSubscribed: true, notificationType: type}`. Without one: any element with `isSubscribed: true`. `$elemMatch` makes sure both conditions hold for the **same** element.

### New metrics for old accounts

```rust
for metric in NOTIFICATION_TYPES.iter().filter(|m| **m != TEMPERATURE) {
    let marker = format!("switched-on:{metric}");
    if self.migrations.find_one(doc! { "_id": &marker }).await?.is_some() {
        continue;
    }
    // for every document with temperature on and no entry for this metric,
    // $push { notificationType: metric, isSubscribed: true }
    // then store the marker
}
```

It runs at every start:

1. For each alertable metric except `temperature`, look for the marker `switched-on:{metric}` in `migrations`. Found: skip.
2. Else, for every document with temperature on and no entry for this metric, push `{notificationType: metric, isSubscribed: true}`.
3. Store the marker.

A stored choice always wins: a document that already has the metric is not touched. Accounts that subscribed before the metric existed get it on, **once**. A failure is only logged.

---

## Manual alert: `trigger`

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant C as Caller
        participant H as trigger_alert
        participant A as Alerts::trigger
        participant T as TwinDirectory
        participant R as Redis
        participant P as Push
        C->>H: POST /trigger {buildingName, message, type, notificationType}
        H->>A: trigger(…, claims.raw, Audience::of(claims))
        A->>A: message defaults to Manual Alert Triggered
        A->>A: type must parse as Severity, default danger
        A->>A: buildingName required
        A->>T: domains_for_building(building, caller's header)
        T-->>A: domains
        A->>A: permitted_by(domains, audience)
        loop each permitted domain
            A->>R: PUBLISH notifications, title CrowdVision Alert
            A->>P: to_domain(notification, domain, notificationType)
        end
        A-->>H: Ok
        H-->>C: 200 {success, message: Notification sent}

The code, in `Alerts::trigger`:

```rust
let permitted = permitted_by(&domains, audience);
if permitted.is_empty() && !domains.is_empty() {
    return Err(DomainError::Forbidden("Not a member of any domain for this building".to_string()));
}
```

`GET /domain/{building}` does **not** limit its answer to the caller's domains, so `trigger` filters it itself.

| Building's domains | Caller's domains | Result |
|---|---|---|
| `eng`, `finance` | `eng` | 200, delivered to `eng` only |
| `finance` | `eng` | 403 `Not a member of any domain for this building` |
| none | `eng` | 200, nothing delivered |
| `finance` | admin | 200, delivered to `finance` |

- **No cooldown**: every call delivers.
- `type` is a severity: `info`, `warning` or `danger`. Anything else is `400 type must be one of: info, warning, danger`.
- `notificationType` is optional. Absent, push reaches every subscriber of the domain with any switch on.
- A lookup failure is `500`.
- `digital-twin` calls `/trigger` as `system:digital-twin` when a registration fails, so its alert reaches every domain of the building.

The breach path takes no caller. It looks up owners with the system identity, whose memberships are empty, and does not filter by audience. The audience filter lives in `trigger` and the handlers only. Moving it into the shared `fan_out` would silence every breach.

---

## Routes

Every field and status code: [Notification API](../api/index.html?spec=notification.yaml) (OpenAPI).

All behind the edge at `/notification/*`. Every route except the public ones needs the `x-gateway-claims` header with a non-blank `accountName`, else `401`.

| Method | Path | Who may call | What it does | Answer |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| `GET` | `/health`, `/metrics` | Anyone | Probe, Prometheus | `200` |
| `GET` | `/public-key` | Anyone | The VAPID public key a browser needs to create a push subscription | `{publicVapidKey}` |
| `POST` | `/subscribe` | Signed-in account | Store this device. With a domain, also switch preferences on | `201 {success: true}` |
| `GET` | `/preferences`, `/preferences/{accountName}` | Signed-in account | The caller's own preferences. The path name is ignored | `{success, accountPreferences}` |
| `POST` | `/preferences` | Member of the named domain | Set one or more switches | `{success: true}` |
| `POST` | `/trigger` | Signed-in account | Manual alert for a building, no cooldown | `{success, message: "Notification sent"}` |

```rust
fn require_membership(claims: &GatewayClaims, domain_name: &str) -> Result<(), DomainError> {
    if Audience::of(claims).permits(domain_name) {
        return Ok(());
    }
    Err(DomainError::Forbidden(format!("Not a member of domain {domain_name}")))
}
```

The membership check is the same `Audience` as everywhere else. So an `admin` or a `system:` caller may change preferences for any domain.

### Errors

`DomainError` becomes `{type, message}`:

| Variant | Status | `type` |
|---|---|---|
| `Validation` | 400 | `Validation Error` |
| `Unauthorized` | 401 | `Unauthorized Error` |
| `Forbidden` | 403 | `Forbidden Error` |
| `NotFound` | 404 | `Not Found Error` |
| `Internal` | 500 | `Internal Server Error`. The message is generic, the cause only logged |

A client over 300 requests in 60 s gets `429`. The client IP is the **rightmost** `x-forwarded-for` entry, the one the edge appended, so a forged left entry cannot reset the count.

---

## `main.rs` and configuration

Startup order:

1. Connect to MongoDB and create the indexes. Failure stops the service.
2. Connect to Redis. Failure stops the service.
3. Run `switch_on_new_metrics_once`. Failure is logged only.
4. Spawn the Kafka listener. A broker down at start is not fatal: the listener retries every 5 s.
5. Serve HTTP with graceful shutdown on `Ctrl+C` or `SIGTERM`.

The wiring is worth reading once:

```rust
let push = Arc::new(Push::new(subscriptions.clone(), stored_preferences.clone(), sender));
let preferences = Arc::new(Preferences::new(subscriptions, stored_preferences));
let alerts = Arc::new(Alerts::new(
    bus.clone(), bus,
    Arc::new(TwinDirectory::new(twin_url)),
    push,
    Arc::new(SystemClock),
));
```

`bus` is passed **twice**: once as the `NotificationBus`, once as the `Cooldown`. The same Redis connection plays both roles.

| Variable | Default | What it is |
|---|---|---|
| `MONGO_URI` | `mongodb://localhost:27017/notificationdb` | The database. Its name comes from the URI path, else `notificationdb` |
| `REDIS_URL` | `redis://localhost:6379` | Bell channel `notifications` and cooldown keys |
| `KAFKA_BROKERS` | `kafka:9092` | Source of `alerts`. Target of `alerts.dlq` |
| `DIGITAL_TWIN_URL` | `http://localhost:3000` | Owner lookup `GET /domain/{building}` |
| `VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY`, `VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY` | empty | Push signing keys. Empty: every push fails and is logged, nothing panics |
| `PORT` | `3000` | HTTP port |

---

## What it stores

MongoDB, two collections plus one bookkeeping collection:

| Collection | One document per | Fields | Indexes |
|---|---|---|---|
| `webpushsubscriptions` | Device | `accountName`, `endpoint`, `keys {p256dh, auth}` | unique `endpoint`. unique `accountName + endpoint`. `accountName` |
| `notificationsubscriptions` | Account and domain | `accountName`, `domainName`, `preferences [{notificationType, isSubscribed}]`, `createdAt` | unique `accountName + domainName`. `domainName` |
| `migrations` | One-time change | `_id` like `switched-on:airQuality`, `at` | |

Redis holds only cooldown keys, value `"1"`, expiry 300 s. Nothing else is kept: alerts are not stored.

---

## Tests

```bash
just test notification               # unit, no infra
just test notification-integration   # tests/*.rs against throwaway Kafka, Mongo, Redis
```

| Where | What it checks |
|---|---|
| `service/alerts.rs` | Every outcome. Cooldown per field. No domain is never published. A breach compared to `schemas/fixtures/notification.json` |
| `service/push.rs` | All devices of all named accounts. `Gone` is deleted. Other failures are kept |
| `service/preferences.rs` | The same endpoint once. The same type not duplicated. The later write wins. Reads only own records |
| `http_api/controllers.rs` | Every route without claims is `401`. The body account is ignored. Membership `403`s. Admin and system reach. Every fixture request is accepted and every rejection refused with its reason |
| `driven/twin.rs` | Encoding, 2 s timeout, cache, no cache for empty or failed, one retry on 5xx, none on 404, one request for 5 concurrent misses |
| `driven/push.rs` | 201 delivered, 404 and 410 gone, other statuses failed, timeout, VAPID header, empty keys never panic |
| `alert_listener.rs` | The watermark commits only a contiguous run |
| `tests/alerts_flow.rs` | A breach produced while the service is off is still delivered on return. A second breach in cooldown publishes nothing. A record that can never be handled lands on `alerts.dlq` |
| `tests/persistence.rs` | Indexes and queries on a real Mongo. New metrics switched on exactly once |
| `tests/architecture_fitness.rs` | Layer imports. The claims header name |

---

## Gaps

- **`alerts.dlq` has no reader.** Parked records stay there until the topic's retention removes them.
- **The cooldown check is not atomic.** Read, deliver, then set: two copies of one breach handled together both deliver.
- **A long breach alerts every 5 minutes.** `telemetry` sends one record per breaching reading, and nothing marks the end of a breach.
- **Preference writes are two steps.** A crash between `$pull` and `$push` loses that switch, which then reads as off.
- **A forbidden domain on `/subscribe` still stores the device.** The answer is `403`, which can read as "nothing stored".
- **The owner cache and its gates are never emptied.** Each building name ever looked up keeps an entry and a lock for the life of the process.
- **The push contact address is fixed in code.** The VAPID `sub` claim is `mailto:admin@crowdvision.com`, with no setting to change it.
